  "conclusion": "The read endpoint was never the problem. `rollbackBarIndex` does not filter the returned list (absent / -1 / 0 returned identical bodies on a long-committed element), and `rollbackIndex` reports the element's own rollback-bar position. The browser-inserted feature genuinely was not in the workspace: waiting ~4 minutes did not commit it and a page reload did. A REST-added feature on the same element was visible immediately, which is the control that rules out a caching artefact on the read side.",
  "limits": [
    "One element, one document, one custom feature type. Not repeated across documents or URL forms.",
    "The mechanism inside the Onshape client (pending edit log flushed on reload, versus a delayed microversion push) is NOT established; only the observed behaviour is.",
    "The reproduction used the page reload as the commit trigger. Whether an explicit save or a tab switch would do the same was not tested.",
    "REST calls spent on the whole follow-up: 10 (the authorized budget), of which the refusal probe consumed 0 quota because 4xx is not counted."
  ],
